### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants a generic solution for the Kth largest value. This generalizes the second-largest pattern and tests parameterization and edge case handling.

### How do you find the Kth largest element?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class KthLargest {
    public static Optional<Integer> findKthLargest(
            List<Integer> list, int k) {
        if (k < 1) throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "K must be >= 1, got: " + k);

        return list.stream()
            .distinct()
            .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
            .skip(k - 1)
            .findFirst();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> amounts = List.of(
            50000, 125000, 75000, 125000, 90000, 60000);

        for (int k = 1; k <= 6; k++) {
            Optional<Integer> result = findKthLargest(amounts, k);
            System.out.printf("K=%d: %s%n", k,
                result.map(v -> "Rs." + v).orElse("N/A"));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
K=1: Rs.125000
K=2: Rs.90000
K=3: Rs.75000
K=4: Rs.60000
K=5: Rs.50000
K=6: N/A
```

### How do you find the Kth smallest?

Replace reverseOrder with natural ascending order.

```java
public static Optional<Integer> findKthSmallest(
        List<Integer> list, int k) {
    return list.stream()
        .distinct()
        .sorted(Comparator.naturalOrder())
        .skip(k - 1)
        .findFirst();
}
```

### What is the efficient approach for large lists?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class KthLargestHeap {
    public static Optional<Integer> findKthLargest(
            List<Integer> list, int k) {
        if (k < 1) throw new IllegalArgumentException("K >= 1");

        TreeSet<Integer> topK = new TreeSet<>();
        for (int n : list) {
            topK.add(n);
            if (topK.size() > k) {
                topK.pollFirst();  // remove smallest
            }
        }
        return topK.size() == k
            ? Optional.of(topK.first())
            : Optional.empty();
    }
}
```

This bounded set approach is O(n log k), which beats O(n log n) when K is much smaller than n.

### How do you handle edge cases?

```java
// K exceeds distinct count: empty
Optional<Integer> beyond = findKthLargest(List.of(100, 200), 5);
System.out.println(beyond.isPresent());  // false

// K = 1: same as max
Optional<Integer> max = findKthLargest(List.of(100, 200), 1);
System.out.println(max.get());  // 200

// Empty list: empty for any K
Optional<Integer> empty = findKthLargest(List.of(), 1);
System.out.println(empty.isPresent());  // false
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using skip(k) instead of skip(k-1).

```java
// WRONG: skip(3) skips positions 1, 2, 3 and returns position 4
Optional<Integer> wrong = list.stream().distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(3).findFirst();  // 4th largest!

// CORRECT: skip(k-1) for the Kth position
Optional<Integer> correct = list.stream().distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(2).findFirst();  // 3rd largest
```

Mistake: forgetting distinct when duplicates exist.

```java
// WRONG: if max appears 3 times, K=2 still gives max
Optional<Integer> wrong = List.of(100, 100, 100, 50).stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println(wrong.get());  // 100 (not 50!)

// CORRECT: distinct collapses duplicate values
Optional<Integer> correct = List.of(100, 100, 100, 50).stream()
    .distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println(correct.get());  // 50
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you find all elements at the Kth rank?" Find the Kth value, then filter the original list for that value.
- "What is the time complexity?" O(n log n) for sort, or O(n log k) for heap.
- "How do you find the Kth largest per department?" Group first, then apply the pattern within each group.
